/* book exam traps: dB HL is not dB SPL. A reading of 40 dB HL means 40 dB above the normal threshold at that frequency, not a fixed pressure, because the reference pressure changes with frequency. | Air conduction and bone conduction are not two names for the same test. Bone conduction skips the outer and middle ear, so an air-bone gap points to a conductive component. | Masking noise is delivered to the non-test ear. Sending it to the test ear raises the threshold and gives a false reading. | The Hughson-Westlake rule is 10 dB down after a heard tone and 5 dB up after a miss. Reversing these steps gives the wrong threshold. | SRT and the word recognition score are different numbers. SRT is the 50% speech level; the word score is a percentage at a chosen comfortable level. */
