/* book: lead: An endoscope lets a doctor look inside a body cavity through a natural opening or a small cut. It pipes light in along one bundle of glass fibres and brings the picture back along a second, ordered bundle, or along a tiny camera chip at the tip. Rigid tubes with glass rod lenses serve joints and the abdomen; flexible fibre-optic and video scopes reach the stomach and the colon. The same instrument */
/* book Figure 6.2.1: The two bundles of a flexible endoscope. The illumination bundle needs no order; the image bundle keeps every fibre in the same place at both ends, so the picture survives. Light stays in each fibre by total internal reflection (θc ≈ 78° for indices 1.50 and 1.47), and the worked example gives 707 µ */
