/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An open cavity is dark and closed.
   - The rigid endoscope is a straight metal tube, a few millimetres across, holding a relay of glass rod lenses.
   - Rigid scopes go where a straight path exists: through the abdominal wall (laparoscopy), into a knee or shoulder joint (arthroscopy), through the urethra into the bladder (cystoscopy).
   - Light travels along a glass fibre by total internal reflection.
   - θc is the critical angle, measured from the normal to the fibre wall; n₁ is the refractive index of the core; n₂ is the refractive index of the cladding, with n₂ < n₁.
   - A flexible endoscope uses two separate bundles.
   - An objective lens at the tip focuses the tissue onto the face of the image bundle, and an eyepiece or camera magnifies what comes out.
   - To aim the tip, two or four thin cables run down the shaft; a knob at the handle pulls one pair against the other and bends a springy section at the tip.
   - The video endoscope removes the eyepiece.
   - A working channel runs the length of the shaft, typically 1.2 mm to 3.8 mm wide.
*/
