/* book exam traps: Swapping the two bundles: the incoherent bundle only carries light, the coherent bundle carries the image because its fibres keep the same order at both ends. | Placing the lamp at the tip of a fibre-optic scope. Its lamp stays outside and light comes down the light bundle; only cable-free designs such as the capsule, and some modern video scopes, carry small LEDs at the tip. | Treating a bigger fibre core as better resolution. A bigger core carries more light but resolves less, because resolution is set by the spacing of cores, core plus cladding. | Assuming every endoscope can be autoclaved. Steam at 134 °C destroys fibre bundles, seals and sensors, so flexible scopes are disinfected chemically instead. | Forgetting that a rigid scope sees only along a straight line, which is why it cannot do gastroscopy or colonoscopy. */
